<!doctype man system>

<man>

<!-- ======================================================================

     class

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=class.n>

<title>
class n 2.0 Tclpp "Tclpp Built-In Commands"
</title>

<name>
class \- Define a class
</name>

<synopsis>
<bf>class</bf> <it>name</it> ?<bf>:</bf> <it>base-classes</it>? <it>definition</it>
</synopsis>

<description>
This command is used to define a class <it>name</it> and its
<it>definition</it> of data members and functions. After a class has been
defined it can be used to instantiate objects. Each object will allocate
memory to hold all its variables in its own separate namespace such that
they will not interfere with other objects. Eventhough they all share the
same methods.
</description>

<sect heading=OBJECTS>
After each class definition, a function is created which name equals the
name of the class that can be used to create instances with. The first
argument of that function is the name of the instance, followed by any 
arguments which are passed to the constructor of that class. An instance
is deleted by invoking the <bf>delete</bf> method on that instance.
<newlines>
Instances can be created dynamically as well, that is, the name of the
instance is constructed by the system and is surely unique. That can
be accomplished by the <bf>new</bf>(n) command. 
Symmetrically to this command is a <bf>delete</bf>(n) command
as well. 
</sect>

<sect heading=VARIABLES>
A class may define variables to hold the data of a certain instance. Each
variable gets allocated its own memory per instance, such that the data
from one object does not interfere with the data of another object. A
variable is defined by the <bf>variable</bf> keyword defined in the
<it>definition</it> section. The following variable types as supported:
<dl>

<dh><bf>variable array</bf> <it>name</it> </dh>
<dt>Define a variable as a Tcl array.</dt>

<dh><bf>variable scalar</bf> <it>name</it> </dh>
<dt>Define a variable as a Tcl scalar variable. This is the Tcl string which 
can be represented as a string, list, integer or
floating point.</dt>

<dh><bf>variable</bf> <it>classname name</it> </dh>
<dt>Define a variable as an instance of a class.</dt>

</dl>
A special scalar variable, <bf>this</bf>, is predefined when an instance
is created. This variable holds the name of instance itself and is
accessible in the class methods as such.
</sect>

<sect heading=METHODS>
A method defined in the <it>definition</it> is just like a regular Tcl
function. All argument and variable (local and global) handling as defined
by a Tcl function apply to a class method as well. There is however one
exception. The class variables are accessible automatically. There is 
no need to import them explicitely.
<newlines>
A method can be invoked through instances only be specifying the instance
followed by the method and any arguments. Any class variable altered in this
function has effect on the associated instance variable only.
<newlines>
A special method, the constructor, is invoked automatically when an instance
is invoked, This method bears the same name as the class. This method may
accept arguments, but it is best to keep them defaulted in case of inheritance.
With inheritance, the constructors of all base classes are invoked without
any argument specified !
<newlines>
Similarly, another special method, the destructor, is invoked automatically
when an instance is deleted. This method bears the same name as the class,
prepended by the `<bf>~</bf>' symbol. This method does not accept any
arguments whatsoever.
</sect>

<sect heading=INHERITANCE>
A class can inherit methods and variables from other classes (the base or
parent classes) by specifying these <it>base-classes</it> before the
class <it>definition</it>. To the user of the class it appears as if these
inherited variables and methods were defined in the class itself. More
than one base class can be inherited. If a base class is inherited multiple
times (e.g. directly or indirectly through the base classes), that base
class will be considered as one base class only (diamond shaped inheritance).
</sect>

<sect heading=VIRTUAL>
Methods defined in base classes can be overridden in derived classes, only
if they have been prefixed with the <bf>virtual</bf> keyword. When such a
method is overridden, any invocation of that method (even the ones invoked
within the base class) will use that method.
</sect>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

<seealso>
classinfo(n), delete(n), new(n)
</seealso>

</manpage>

<!-- ======================================================================

     classinfo

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=classinfo.n>

<title>
classinfo n 2.0 Tclpp "Tclpp Built-In Commands"
</title>

<name>
classinfo \- Obtain information about classes
</name>

<synopsis>
<bf>classinfo</bf> <it>option</it> ?<it>arg ...</it>?
</synopsis>

<description>
This command provides information about classes. The legal options (which may 
be abbreviated) are:
<dl>

<dh><bf>classinfo classes</bf> ?<it>pattern</it>?</dh>
<dt>Returns a list of fully qualified class names known to the system at
that point. If <it>pattern</it> is specified, only those names are
returned which match the <it>pattern</it>.</dt>

<dh><bf>classinfo heritage</bf> <it>class</it></dh>
<dt>Returns a list of the direct base classes of the <it>class</it>. These
are only the direct parents of this <it>class</it>, not its entire
lineage.</dt>

<dh><bf>classinfo methods</bf> <it>class</it></dh>
<dt>Returns a list of all the methods specified in this <it>class</it>.</dt>

<dh><bf>classinfo variables</bf> <it>class</it></dh>
<dt>Returns a list of all the variables and associated type specified in this
<it>class</it>.</dt>

</dl>

</description>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

<seealso>
class(n), objectinfo(n)
</seealso>

</manpage>

<!-- ======================================================================

     objectinfo

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=objectinfo.n>

<title>
objectinfo n 2.0 Tclpp "Tclpp Built-In Commands"
</title>

<name>
objectinfo \- Obtain information about objects (instances)
</name>

<synopsis>
<bf>objectinfo</bf> <it>object</it> <it>option</it> ?<it>arg ...</it>?
</synopsis>

<description>
This command provides information about objects. The legal options (which may 
be abbreviated) are:
<dl>

<dh><bf>objectinfo</bf> <it>object</it> <bf>isA</bf></dh>
<dt>Returns the fully qualified class name (starting with <bf>::</bf>) of
the <it>object</it>.</dt>

<dh><bf>objectinfo</bf> <it>object</it> <bf>isKindOf</bf> <it>class</it></dh>
<dt>Returns <it>1</it> if the <it>object</it> is (derived from) <it>class</it>.
Returns <it>0</it> otherwise.</dt>

</dl>

</description>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

<seealso>
class(n), classinfo(n)
</seealso>

</manpage>

<!-- ======================================================================

     delete

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=delete.n>

<title>
delete n 2.0 Tclpp "Tclpp Built-In Commands"
</title>

<name>
delete \- Delete an instance of a class
</name>

<synopsis>
<bf>delete</bf> <it>object</it> <newline>
<it>object</it> <bf>delete</bf> 
</synopsis>

<description>
Delete the object entirely (including all its contents) and invoke the
destructor of the object. The destructor of all its base classes and
of all its variables will be called as well.
<newlines>
Two methods are supported. The first is a command called <bf>delete</bf>
and the other is calling an instance procedure called <bf>delete</bf>. Both
are equivalent.
</description>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

<seealso>
class(n), new(n)
</seealso>

</manpage>

<!-- ======================================================================

     new

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=new.n>

<title>
new n 2.0 Tclpp "Tclpp Built-In Commands"
</title>

<name>
new \- Create a new, uniquely named instance of a class
</name>

<synopsis>
<bf>new</bf> <it>class</it> ?<it>constructor-arg ...</it>? <newline>
<it>class</it> <it>object</it> ?<it>constructor-arg ...</it>?
</synopsis>

<description>
Construct a new instance of a class and return the instance name created. 
The constructor of the class will be invoked. The constructors of the
base classes and all its variables will be called as well.
<newlines>
There are two methods to create an instance. The first is using the 
<bf>new</bf> command which will assign a unique instance name, while the
other is using the class-name command which requires a user defined
name. In the latter case, the user has to ensure the uniqueness of the
instance name.
</description>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

<seealso>
class(n), delete(n)
</seealso>

</manpage>

<!-- ======================================================================

     tclppvars

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=tclppvars.n>

<title>
tclppvars n 2.0 Tclpp "Tclpp Built-In Commands"
</title>

<name>
tclppvars \- Variables used by Tclpp
</name>

<description>
The following global variables are created and managed by the Tclpp library.
All these variables should be treated as read-only by application specific
code and by users.
<dl>

<dh><bf>tclpp_patchLevel</bf></dh>
<dt>Returns the current version and patch level information as a string. For 
example, <bf>2.0b2</bf> represents the second beta release of release 2.0.</dt>

<dh><bf>tclpp_version</bf></dh>
<dt>Return the major and minor release number of the current Tclpp release.
This does not include any patch level information.</dt>

</dl>
</description>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

</manpage>

<!-- ======================================================================

     tclppsh

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=tclppsh.1>

<title>
tclppsh 1 2.0 Tclpp "Tclpp Applications"
</title>

<name>
tclppsh \- Simple Tcl interpreter shell extended with Tclpp commands
</name>

<synopsis>
<bf>tclppsh</bf> ?<it>fileName arg ...</it>? <newline>
</synopsis>

<description>
The <bf>tclppsh</bf> is a shell-like application that reads Tcl and Tclpp
commands from its standard input or from a file and evaluates them.
If invoked with no arguments then it runs interactively, reading commands
from standard input and printing command results and error messages
to standard output.
It runs until the <bf>exit</bf> command is invoked or until it
reaches end-of-file on its standard input.
</description>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

<seealso>
wishpp(1), tclsh(1), wish(1)
</seealso>

</manpage>

<!-- ======================================================================

     wishpp

     ====================================================================== -->

<manpage basename=wishpp.1>

<title>
wishpp 1 2.0 Wishpp "Tclpp Applications"
</title>

<name>
wishpp \- Simple Tcl windowing shell extended with Tclpp commands
</name>

<synopsis>
<bf>wishpp</bf> ?<it>fileName arg ...</it>? <newline>
</synopsis>

<description>
The <bf>wishpp</bf> is a windowing shell application that reads Tcl, Tk
and Tclpp commands from its standard input or from a file and evaluates them.
If invoked with no arguments then it runs interactively, reading commands
from standard input and printing command results and error messages
to standard output.
It runs until the <bf>exit</bf> command is invoked or until it
reaches end-of-file on its standard input.
</description>

<author>
Stefan Sinnige (ssinnige@geocities.com)
</author>

<seealso>
tclppsh(1), tclsh(1), wish(1)
</seealso>

</manpage>

</man>

